>From a study I have done on affymatrix chips (HU95A). the pooled sample
does reproduce the average of individual samples. I haven't got
time to write the paper. but I will be happen to sent you the data that
demonstrate this. By pooling, the biological variation are averaged, the
only power you lose is from the technical variation, which is quite low
with affymatrix chips. 

As I know, similar but larger studies on pooling is undergone at U. Of Wisconsin
on mice and rats. I expect the similar results will be shown.

Pooling, however, is more suitable for mice studies(animal in general)
than human, as the mices being pooled are often similar from same strain.
Also, pooling should have some other advantage in your case as 
the signal/noise ratio should be higher for pooled sample (more RNA)
compared to the individuals. 

Kenny

